---
// The architecture in one picture: what decides, what runs, what is recorded,
// and the fact that the recording is what the deciding reads. Inline SVG so it
// inherits the page's theme tokens and needs no script.
//
// Two things the arrows are deliberately careful about. The router fans out to
// every stage rather than pointing at the first one -- it picks whichever is
// legal, and drawing a sequence would claim a fixed order the code does not
// have. And `train` has no arrow into RECORD because it writes a checkpoint,
// not a dataset; giving it one would be tidier and false.
---

<figure class="diagram">
  <svg viewBox="0 0 880 462" role="img" aria-labelledby="loop-title loop-desc">
    <title id="loop-title">The labeling loop</title>
    <desc id="loop-desc">
      Three bands. Planning picks exactly one stage to run next. The pipeline
      runs it, and the stages that produce rows write them into versioned
      datasets, all of which are indexed by a registry that planning reads to
      decide again. One stage in the middle of the pipeline is a person.
    </desc>

    <rect x="8" y="14" width="822" height="112" rx="10" class="band"></rect>
    <rect x="8" y="170" width="822" height="112" rx="10" class="band"></rect>
    <rect x="8" y="326" width="822" height="120" rx="10" class="band"></rect>

    <text x="26" y="38" class="band-label">PLAN</text>
    <text x="26" y="194" class="band-label">RUN</text>
    <text x="26" y="350" class="band-label">RECORD</text>

    <!-- plan -->
    <g class="node plan">
      <rect x="90" y="46" width="170" height="58" rx="8"></rect>
      <text x="175" y="72">select_scene</text>
      <text x="175" y="90" class="sub">what to label next</text>
    </g>
    <g class="node plan">
      <rect x="300" y="46" width="180" height="58" rx="8"></rect>
      <text x="390" y="72">router</text>
      <text x="390" y="90" class="sub">what to run next</text>
    </g>
    <g class="node plan">
      <rect x="520" y="46" width="200" height="58" rx="8"></rect>
      <text x="620" y="72">optimize_prompt</text>
      <text x="620" y="90" class="sub">which prompt wins</text>
    </g>

    <!-- the router picks one of the run stages, not the first of them -->
    <g class="fan">
      <path d="M390,104 L390,146"></path>
      <path d="M390,146 L182,196" marker-end="url(#tip-fan)"></path>
      <path d="M390,146 L316,196" marker-end="url(#tip-fan)"></path>
      <path d="M390,146 L466,196" marker-end="url(#tip-fan)"></path>
      <path d="M390,146 L612,196" marker-end="url(#tip-fan)"></path>
      <path d="M390,146 L742,196" marker-end="url(#tip-fan)"></path>
    </g>
    <text x="512" y="136" class="note fan-note">picks exactly one</text>

    <!-- run -->
    <g class="node run">
      <rect x="120" y="202" width="118" height="48" rx="8"></rect>
      <text x="179" y="232">geometry</text>
    </g>
    <g class="node run">
      <rect x="256" y="202" width="118" height="48" rx="8"></rect>
      <text x="315" y="232">naming</text>
    </g>
    <g class="node human">
      <rect x="392" y="202" width="152" height="48" rx="8"></rect>
      <text x="468" y="225">gold · review</text>
      <text x="468" y="242" class="sub">a person</text>
    </g>
    <g class="node run">
      <rect x="562" y="202" width="110" height="48" rx="8"></rect>
      <text x="617" y="232">train</text>
    </g>
    <g class="node run">
      <rect x="690" y="202" width="118" height="48" rx="8"></rect>
      <text x="749" y="232">score</text>
    </g>

    <!-- record -->
    <g class="node data">
      <rect x="240" y="358" width="150" height="48" rx="8"></rect>
      <text x="315" y="388">labels</text>
    </g>
    <g class="node data">
      <rect x="430" y="358" width="140" height="48" rx="8"></rect>
      <text x="500" y="388">registry</text>
    </g>
    <g class="node data">
      <rect x="600" y="358" width="180" height="48" rx="8"></rect>
      <text x="690" y="388">evaluations</text>
    </g>

    <g class="flow">
      <path d="M179,250 L272,354" marker-end="url(#tip)"></path>
      <path d="M315,250 L315,354" marker-end="url(#tip)"></path>
      <path d="M468,250 L356,354" marker-end="url(#tip)"></path>
      <path d="M749,250 L700,354" marker-end="url(#tip)"></path>
      <path d="M390,382 L426,382" marker-end="url(#tip)"></path>
      <path d="M598,382 L574,382" marker-end="url(#tip)"></path>
      <path
        class="feedback"
        d="M500,406 L500,428 L850,428 L850,75 L724,75"
        marker-end="url(#tip-feedback)"></path>
    </g>
    <text x="838" y="304" class="note feedback-note">read back</text>

    <defs>
      <marker
        id="tip"
        viewBox="0 0 10 10"
        refX="9"
        refY="5"
        markerWidth="6"
        markerHeight="6"
        orient="auto-start-reverse"
      >
        <path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" class="tip"></path>
      </marker>
      <marker
        id="tip-fan"
        viewBox="0 0 10 10"
        refX="9"
        refY="5"
        markerWidth="5"
        markerHeight="5"
        orient="auto-start-reverse"
      >
        <path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" class="tip-fan"></path>
      </marker>
      <marker
        id="tip-feedback"
        viewBox="0 0 10 10"
        refX="9"
        refY="5"
        markerWidth="6"
        markerHeight="6"
        orient="auto-start-reverse"
      >
        <path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" class="tip-feedback"></path>
      </marker>
    </defs>
  </svg>
  <figcaption>
    Planning picks one stage, the pipeline runs it, and whatever rows it
    produces become a new immutable version — indexed by the registry that
    planning reads to decide again. <code>train</code> is the one stage with no
    arrow down: it writes a checkpoint, not a dataset.
  </figcaption>
</figure>

<style>
  .diagram {
    margin: 2rem 0;
  }
  .diagram svg {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    display: block;
  }
  .band {
    fill: var(--surface-2);
    stroke: var(--border);
  }
  .band-label {
    fill: var(--ink-3);
    font:
      600 11px var(--sans);
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
  }
  .node rect {
    fill: var(--surface);
    stroke: var(--border);
    stroke-width: 1.5;
  }
  .node text {
    text-anchor: middle;
    fill: var(--ink);
    font:
      600 14px var(--sans);
  }
  .node text.sub {
    fill: var(--ink-3);
    font:
      400 11px var(--sans);
  }
  .node.plan rect {
    stroke: var(--accent);
  }
  .node.human rect {
    stroke: var(--accent-2);
    stroke-width: 2;
  }
  .node.data rect {
    stroke: var(--accent-3);
  }
  .flow path {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--ink-3);
    stroke-width: 1.5;
  }
  .flow path.feedback {
    stroke: var(--accent-3);
    stroke-dasharray: 5 4;
  }
  /* Lighter than the data flow: this is the choice, not the movement. */
  .fan path {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--accent);
    stroke-width: 1;
    opacity: 0.55;
  }
  .tip {
    fill: var(--ink-3);
  }
  .tip-fan {
    fill: var(--accent);
  }
  .tip-feedback {
    fill: var(--accent-3);
  }
  .note {
    fill: var(--ink-3);
    font:
      400 11px var(--sans);
    text-anchor: middle;
  }
  /* Classes, not text-anchor attributes: a presentation attribute loses to the
     `.note` rule above, so it would silently do nothing. */
  .note.feedback-note {
    fill: var(--accent-3);
    text-anchor: end;
  }
  .note.fan-note {
    fill: var(--accent);
  }
</style>
